# aGTM serverside GTM Client Template

This is the aGTM Client Template to use it with the serverside Google Tag Manager.

## What is new in 1.4.3

**Fix — the "Use env Parameter" switch never did anything.** The container table
defines the column as `gtm_use`, but the code read `val.gtm_env`, a column that
does not exist. `env` was therefore never set and the GTM container URL never
carried the environment parameters, whatever the switch was set to. Present
since the column was introduced (2025-09-24), so the switch has never worked.

**New — the switch is now a per-container choice** (column renamed to "URL
Parameters") and it also accepts a **variable**:

| Option | What is appended to the container URL |
|---|---|
| `no` | nothing (default) |
| `env from URL` | `gtm_auth`, `gtm_preview`, `gtm_cookies_win` from the aGTM.js request, if present |
| `all from URL` | every query parameter of the request except aGTM's own `id` and `c` |
| *a variable* | if it resolves to none of the three above, **its value IS the parameter string** |

So a Constant variable holding `&gtm_auth=ABC123xyz&gtm_preview=env-1` appends
exactly that; a leading `?`/`&` is optional. There is deliberately no second
column: one field carries both the choice and, in that case, the value.

Guardrails, because that value goes verbatim into the address the page loads GTM
from:

- A resolved value only counts if it **looks** like a parameter string (`k=v`
  with a non-empty name). A variable returning a container id, a stale `yes` or
  an error message is ignored and **written to the container log** — a renamed or
  failing variable would otherwise change which environment loads without leaving
  a trace anywhere.
- An **empty** value means "not configured" and appends nothing. That is what an
  untouched row looks like, and it is not logged as an error.
- Values taken from the request are URL-encoded, so a parameter cannot smuggle in
  further parameters. A parameter the caller repeated (`?a=1&a=2`) is reproduced
  in full, in order — dropping it would silently lose an env setting, and taking
  "the first one" would invent a rule nobody agreed to.
- Repetitions are capped at 10 and the whole string at 1000 characters. The cap
  bounds the loop, not just the output, and the budget is checked *before*
  appending so a parameter is either fully present or absent — a URL cut in the
  middle of a parameter looks valid and is wrong.
- A row still carrying the old boolean `true` (the former "yes") keeps meaning
  the env parameters, so an existing configuration does not change meaning — it
  starts working, which is the point.

`all from URL` forwards whatever a caller puts in the URL into the address the
page loads GTM from. Prefer `env from URL` unless you actually need it.

**Updated — the embedded CMP consent_check codes** were regenerated from the
current adapters; 10 of the 23 had drifted. The most consequential: **Borlabs v2
never returned `true`**, so on a Borlabs-v2 site aGTM never saw a consent result
and GTM never loaded at all. Also refreshed: Consentmanager, CookieFirst, Matomo,
Shopware Acris, Sourcepoint, Usercentrics v2 and v3, Shopify, Shopware 5. Every
one was checked against the **embedded** library (1.4.2) first — they use no
helper it lacks, so all 23 are compatible with the library this Client ships.

**Fix — the client-IP 403 did not stop the response.** `if (clientIP == '')`
sent a 403 and then fell through: the Client went on to build and send the full
library as a second response. `returnResponse()` made that moot on the wire, so
it never showed — but the work happened on every blocked request, and a blocked
visitor could not be observed as blocked. A `return` now ends it there.

**Tests.** The "Tests" tab had one scenario in which every mock and every
assertion was commented out — it ran and checked nothing, and its container
fixture was an object where the field is a table, so the loop it was meant to
exercise could never run. Replaced with 20 scenarios covering the URL-Parameters
column, the `?id=` gate and the routing.

**Not changed: the embedded aGTM library is still 1.4.2.** It predates the
fail-closed consent gate and the escaped cookie/URL-parameter reads. If this
Client serves a configuration with `gtmPurposes`/`gtmServices`/`gtmVendors`
requirements, be aware that an empty consent string is treated as satisfied.

